--TEST--
A `mixed` holding a `Core` instance is got back by naming its class, and the test narrows it to that class — `rule:types/class-reference-sites`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `array<mixed>` is where instances of unrelated classes travel together, and
// `rule:types/unions-and-mixed` makes naming the class the only way back out of one.
// Each element here is an instance a `Core` member built.
array<mixed> $things = [
    Core\Time\Date::at(2026, 9, 17),
    Core\Uri::parse("https://example.test/a?b=c"),
    Core\Regex::match("order 4711 shipped", "\\d+"),
    "a string is no instance",
    7,
];

// The true edge narrows, so the member call needs no `as` and has no throw
// path: the value is a `Core\Time\Date` inside the branch that asked
// (`rule:types/narrowing`).
foreach ($things as mixed $one) {
    if ($one is Core\Time\Date) {
        echo "date ", $one->format("yyyy-MM-dd"), "|\n";
    } else if ($one is Core\Uri) {
        echo "uri ", $one->host(), "|\n";
    } else if ($one is Core\Regex\Match) {
        echo "match ", $one->text(), "|\n";
    } else {
        echo "no core instance|\n";
    }
}

// A `Core` class answers for itself and for nothing else — there is no
// hierarchy under one to walk, so a sibling class and a declared class both
// answer `false` over the same value.
mixed $date = Core\Time\Date::at(2026, 9, 17);
if (!($date is Core\Uri)) {
    echo "a date is no uri|\n";
}
if ($date is Core\Time\Date) {
    echo "a date is a date|\n";
}
--EXPECT--
date 2026-09-17|
uri example.test|
match 4711|
no core instance|
no core instance|
a date is no uri|
a date is a date|
